<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Serves</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>src/invoices/page.abide</code></td><td><code>/invoices</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>src/invoices/[id]/page.abide</code></td><td><code>/invoices/42</code>, as <code>route.params.id</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>src/layout.abide</code></td><td>the chrome for everything below it</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>src/error.abide</code></td><td>where a failure renders, 404 included</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`<a href={url('/invoices/[id]', { id })}>See more</a>`} />

    <Section title="Adding a page">
        <p>The file is the route, so there is no table to keep in step with the filesystem. The same
        app can be served under a sub-path without a link being rewritten.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/add-a-page')}>Routes</a> ·
        <a href={url('/pages/serve-the-app-under-a-sub-path')}>Sub-path mounting</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Layouts that do not rebuild">
        <p>A layout wraps the pages nested under it and does not rebuild when the page inside it
        changes.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/give-pages-the-same-chrome')}>Layouts</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Moving from one page to another">
        <p><code>url()</code> builds an address that survives a rename and a mount path, so a link
        is checked rather than typed. A view transition is turned on by the stylesheet that
        describes it.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/link-to-another-page')}>Links &amp; navigation</a> ·
        <a href={url('/pages/animate-from-one-page-to-the-next')}>View transitions</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="When a page fails">
        <p>A failure renders in one place, and 404 reaches it the same way everything else does
        rather than being a case of its own.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/show-a-page-when-something-fails')}>Error pages</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Streaming the document before the data">
        <p>The document can be flushed before the data lands, with the holes filling as values
        arrive — and the title and social preview are their own decision about what is worth waiting
        for.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/send-the-page-before-the-data-lands')}>Streaming HTML</a> ·
        <a href={url('/pages/set-the-title-and-social-preview')}>Head &amp; metadata</a> ·
        <a href={url('/pages/render-a-document-yourself')}>Manual rendering</a></p>
    </Section>
</Doc>
